00:07:04.580 --> 00:07:12.160
A recent paper argues that intermediate thinking tokens function as prompt augmentation rather than human-like reasoning traces.

00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:20.340
Models trained on corrupted traces still achieve comparable accuracy and trace length shows little correlation with problem difficulty.

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:31.440
The study finds no consistent link between trace validity and solution correctness and reinforcement learning can improve final accuracy while sometimes decreasing trace validity.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.600
Okay, let's pop the hood on agent population scaling.

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:41.520
Everyone talks about more agents as if simply adding instances will produce better collective behavior.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:53.180
In practice, the transition from single agents to populations introduces coordination overhead, communication topology choices, and emergent failure modes that are not visible at small scale.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:08:02.520
At low agent counts, simple voting or sequential handoff works because conflicts remain rare and shared state stays consistent with minimal synchronization.

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:15.760
Once dozens of agents operate concurrently on shared state, message volume grows quadratically unless explicit summarization or hierarchical routing is added to keep each agent's context window manageable.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:29.960
Experiments show that beyond roughly 20 to 30 agents, the dominant cost shifts from individual inference to maintaining consistent world models across the group, often requiring dedicated memory or consensus layers.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:44.420
The practical decision point is whether your workflow benefits from parallel exploration, favoring loose coupling and periodic merge steps, or requires tight consensus, favoring explicit coordination layers and shared memory buses.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:53.980
Teams that skip this analysis usually hit either redundant work or deadlock once population size exceeds the single agent regime they originally tested.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:01.540
When scaling past 30 agents, start by measuring message volume and world model divergence before adding more instances.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:06.280
The first bottleneck is almost always coordination rather than raw compute.

